@page "/components/date-picker"

<PageTitle>Date Picker — Unpoly.Blazor.Shadcn</PageTitle>

<h1 class="doc-h1">Date Picker</h1>
<p class="doc-lede">
    A button that says the date, a popover, and a <a href="/components/calendar">Calendar</a>
    inside it — shadcn's shape, built out of what the platform already has. The day is a real
    radio with a name, so the form posts the date whether or not any script ran; the panel is a
    <code>[popover]</code>, so it opens before one has.
</p>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Basic</h2>
<Example Source="DatePickerBasic.razor"><DatePickerBasic /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Range Picker</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    Two months side by side and a panel that stays open until both ends are ticked. Underneath the
    days are checkboxes with one name: the server takes the min and the max, which is a working
    range picker with JavaScript switched off.
</p>
<Example Source="DatePickerRange.razor"><DatePickerRange /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Date of Birth</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The same picker, narrower, opening on the month someone born thirty years ago would want.
    Upstream draws the caption as two dropdowns for exactly that reason — see
    <a href="/components/calendar">Calendar</a>, where the month and year selects live.
</p>
<Example Source="DatePickerBirthday.razor"><DatePickerBirthday /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Input</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    The date in a text box, with the calendar as a small button riding inside it. Choosing a day
    writes the date into the input, so what posts and what is on screen are one value.
</p>
<Example Source="DatePickerInput.razor"><DatePickerInput /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Time Picker</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    A date and a time are two values, so they are two controls — and <code>type="time"</code>
    brings the right keyboard on a phone, which a text box with a mask does not.
</p>
<Example Source="DatePickerTime.razor"><DatePickerTime /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Natural Language Picker</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    shadcn parses "In 2 days" in the browser and prints what it understood underneath. Parsing is
    the server's job here — it already has to, because the value arrives as text either way — so
    <code>up-validate</code> echoes the reading back, and the calendar button is there for anyone
    who would rather point at a day.
</p>
<Example Source="DatePickerNatural.razor"><DatePickerNatural /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">Calendar</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    When the shape of the month is the information rather than the value, that is
    <a href="/components/calendar">Calendar</a> — a table of days, each one a real radio input.
</p>
<Example Source="CalendarExample.razor"><CalendarExample /></Example>

<h2 class="doc-h2">RTL</h2>
<p class="doc-note">
    Nothing in the markup says "left" or "right": the chevron, the panel's alignment and the
    calendar's own week all follow <code>dir</code>, which is what lets one component serve both.
</p>
<Example Source="DatePickerRtl.razor"><DatePickerRtl /></Example>
